window.__hvBdRegister("hellenica", {"key":"hellenica","sections":[{"i":1,"j":38,"loc":"1.1.1–1.1.37","title":"Athenian victories at Abydus and Cyzicus","summary":"The narrative opens mid-war in the Hellespont: after a skirmish at Rhoeteum, the Athenians fight Mindarus near Abydus until Alcibiades arrives and turns the battle. Alcibiades escapes from Tissaphernes' prison at Sardis, gathers the fleet, and at Cyzicus traps the Peloponnesian ships; Mindarus falls and all the enemy ships are taken except the Syracusan ones, which their crews burn. Pharnabazus consoles and re-equips the defeated, ordering new ships built at Antandrus, while the banished Syracusan generals, led by Hermocrates, hand over command with their troops' loyalty intact. The year closes with Agis' raid on Athens from Decelea, Clearchus sent to Byzantium, and the Carthaginian capture of Selinus and Himera.","ideas":["Alcibiades' arrival decides the battle near Abydus","Cyzicus: Mindarus killed and the Peloponnesian fleet captured","Hippocrates' intercepted letter: ships gone, men starving","Pharnabazus funds a new fleet; Hermocrates' exile and character"],"terms":[{"t":"tithe-duty","d":"The toll the Athenians levied at Chrysopolis on ships sailing out of the Pontus."},{"t":"Decelea","d":"The Attic fort held by the Spartan king Agis, from which he harassed Athens."}]},{"i":39,"j":79,"loc":"1.2.1–1.3.22","title":"Thrasyllus at Ephesus; Calchedon and Byzantium fall","summary":"Thrasyllus campaigns in Ionia, raiding Lydia, but is beaten at Ephesus by the Ephesians, Tissaphernes' levies and the Syracusan crews, who are honoured for their valour. His troops winter with Alcibiades' veterans at Lampsacus and are reconciled after a joint victory over Pharnabazus. The next year the Athenians wall in Calchedon, kill the Spartan governor Hippocrates, and agree terms with Pharnabazus, who undertakes to escort Athenian envoys to the King. At Byzantium, while the governor Clearchus is away, citizens open the gates to Alcibiades; one of them, Anaxilaus, is later acquitted at Sparta on the plea that he saved his starving city.","ideas":["Athenian defeat at Ephesus and Syracusan honours","Veterans refuse to serve with Thrasyllus' defeated men until a shared victory","Calchedon spared in a compact with Pharnabazus; oaths exchanged with Alcibiades","Byzantium betrayed from within; Anaxilaus' defence"],"terms":[{"t":"peltasts","d":"Light-armed troops with small shields, used here alongside hoplites."},{"t":"harmost (governor)","d":"A Lacedaemonian official placed in command of an allied or subject city, like Clearchus at Byzantium."}]},{"i":80,"j":123,"loc":"1.4.1–1.5.21","title":"Alcibiades returns; Cyrus, Lysander and Notium","summary":"Cyrus comes down as lord of the coast to back Sparta, and the Athenian envoys are held for three years. Alcibiades, elected general in absence, sails into Piraeus on the ill-omened Plynteria; the crowd debates whether he was wronged or caused the city's troubles, and he is made general-in-chief and leads the Eleusinian procession by land. Lysander wins Cyrus' favour and a raise in sailors' pay to four obols. While Alcibiades is away, his pilot Antiochus provokes a battle at Notium and loses fifteen ships; Athens blames Alcibiades, chooses ten new generals, and he withdraws to his castle in the Chersonese.","ideas":["Cyrus sent as caranus with the King's backing for Sparta","Divided public opinion on Alcibiades at his homecoming","Lysander's request: an extra obol per sailor","Notium defeat leads to Alcibiades' fall"],"terms":[{"t":"caranus","d":"Persian title for Cyrus' command, glossed by Xenophon as 'lord'."},{"t":"Plynteria","d":"Athenian festival when Athena's statue was veiled and no serious business was done."},{"t":"obol","d":"A small coin; one-sixth of a drachma, the unit of the sailors' daily wage."}]},{"i":124,"j":161,"loc":"1.6.1–1.6.38","title":"Callicratidas, Mytilene, and the Arginusae battle","summary":"Callicratidas replaces Lysander, faces the intrigues of Lysander's friends, and, refusing to wait at Cyrus' gates, raises money from the Milesians and declares Greeks should not toady to barbarians. He storms Methymna but refuses to enslave Greeks, then pens Conon in the harbour of Mytilene; one ship slips out and brings word to Athens. Athens mans a huge relief fleet, even with slaves and knights, and meets Callicratidas off the Arginusae islands. Callicratidas, refusing to flee, falls overboard and the Peloponnesians are routed, but a storm prevents the rescue of the wrecked crews, and Eteonicus escapes from Mytilene by a ruse.","ideas":["Callicratidas' Panhellenic stance: no Greek enslaved while he commands","Conon blockaded at Mytilene and the escape of one ship","Athenian double line versus the Spartan single line at Arginusae","Storm prevents rescue of the shipwrecked"],"terms":[{"t":"nauarch (admiral)","d":"The annual Spartan fleet commander; Lysander's term ends and Callicratidas succeeds."},{"t":"taxiarchs","d":"Athenian infantry officers, here given ships at Arginusae."}]},{"i":162,"j":196,"loc":"1.7.1–1.7.35","title":"Trial of the generals after Arginusae","summary":"The victorious generals are deposed and arraigned for failing to pick up the shipwrecked; Theramenes leads the attack, and after a postponement his party stages mourners at the Apaturia and has Callixeinus propose judging all the generals by a single vote. The crowd shouts down a challenge to the motion's legality, and among the Prytanes only Socrates refuses to put an unlawful question. Euryptolemus argues for separate trials under the decree of Cannonus, explaining that captains including Theramenes and Thrasybulus were assigned the rescue and the storm prevented it. His motion first carries, then loses on a second vote; six generals are executed, and the Athenians soon repent and turn on Callixeinus.","ideas":["Theramenes shifts blame to the generals","Collective verdict by one vote versus lawful separate trials","Socrates alone refuses to put an illegal vote","Execution of six generals and later remorse"],"terms":[{"t":"Prytanes","d":"The presiding committee of the Council, responsible for putting motions to the Assembly."},{"t":"decree of Cannonus","d":"A severe law requiring a person accused of wronging the people to plead in fetters, tried individually."},{"t":"graphē paranomōn (unconstitutional proposal)","d":"The summons Euryptolemus served on Callixeinus for an illegal motion."}]},{"i":197,"j":229,"loc":"2.1.1–2.1.32","title":"Lysander returns and wins at Aegospotami","summary":"Eteonicus crushes a mutiny of starving troops at Chios by killing one reed-bearer and then paying the men. At the allies' and Cyrus' request Lysander returns as vice-admiral in fact commanding the fleet; Cyrus, summoned to his dying father, gives him all his revenues. Lysander takes Lampsacus, and the Athenians anchor opposite at Aegospotami on an open beach; Alcibiades warns them to move to Sestus but is dismissed. For four days Lysander declines battle, and on the fifth he strikes when the Athenians have scattered, capturing almost the whole fleet. Conon escapes to Cyprus, the Paralus carries the news, and the allies vote to kill the Athenian prisoners.","ideas":["Eteonicus suppresses the reed conspiracy","Lysander admiral in all but name; Cyrus' money","Alcibiades' ignored warning","Fleet captured on the beach; Philocles executed"],"terms":[{"t":"Aegospotami","d":"'Goat Rivers', the open shore in the Chersonese where the Athenian fleet was taken."},{"t":"Paralus","d":"An Athenian state trireme, which escaped and brought the news home."}]},{"i":230,"j":263,"loc":"2.2.1–2.3.10","title":"Siege and surrender of Athens","summary":"Lysander sends every Athenian he finds home to hasten famine, and Athens hears the news with a night of wailing, fearing the treatment it gave Melos and others. Blockaded by Pausanias' army and Lysander's fleet, the Athenians hold out while many starve; Theramenes stays with Lysander over three months and then heads an embassy to Sparta. Corinth and Thebes urge destroying the city, but Sparta refuses to enslave a city that served Greece in its greatest perils, imposing terms: the long walls razed, the fleet surrendered, exiles recalled, and Sparta followed in war. The walls come down to flute music, Lysander reduces Samos and sails home with the spoils, and the war ends after twenty-eight and a half years.","ideas":["Athenians fear the fate they inflicted on Melos","Theramenes' long delay with Lysander as famine deepens","Sparta refuses to destroy Athens; terms of peace","Walls demolished as 'the beginning of freedom for Greece'"],"terms":[{"t":"long walls","d":"The fortifications joining Athens to Piraeus, whose destruction was the core of the peace terms."},{"t":"ephors","d":"The five annual magistrates of Sparta who received envoys and handled peace and war; years are dated by them."}]},{"i":264,"j":287,"loc":"2.3.11–2.3.34","title":"The Thirty's terror and Critias' accusation","summary":"The Thirty, chosen to draft a constitution, delay it and first execute informers, then with a Spartan garrison begin killing men of standing. Theramenes objects; Critias replies that a ruling few must guard power as a tyrant would. The Thirty enroll three thousand citizens, disarm everyone else, and decide that each member should seize a resident alien for his property, which Theramenes refuses. Critias then arraigns Theramenes before the Senate as a born traitor, nicknamed 'Buskin' for facing both ways, who turned on the Four Hundred and on the Arginusae generals.","ideas":["From punishing informers to murdering the eminent and metics","Theramenes: an oligarchy of force must not be weaker than its subjects","The roll of the Three Thousand and the disarming of the rest","Critias: treason is worse than open war"],"terms":[{"t":"the Thirty","d":"The oligarchic board that ruled Athens after the surrender with Spartan support."},{"t":"kothornos ('Buskin')","d":"A boot fitting either foot; Critias' nickname for Theramenes as a turncoat."},{"t":"metics (resident aliens)","d":"Free non-citizens living in Athens, targeted by the Thirty for their wealth."}]},{"i":288,"j":309,"loc":"2.3.35–2.3.56","title":"Theramenes' defence and death","summary":"Theramenes answers that the generals accused him first, that he opposed the Thirty only when they began killing innocent men like Leon of Salamis and Niceratus, and that their own violence is what strengthens the exiles. He defends his record against the Four Hundred and says he has always favoured government by those able to serve with horse or shield, against both mob rule and narrow tyranny. When the Senate applauds, Critias posts men with daggers, strikes Theramenes off the roll, and condemns him. Dragged from the altar by Satyrus, Theramenes drinks the hemlock and toasts his 'beloved Critias'; Xenophon admires his composure and wit at death.","ideas":["Injustice, not criticism, makes enemies for the regime","Theramenes' middle constitution of those who bear arms","Critias strikes him off the roll to bypass the Senate","Last words and the kottabos toast"],"terms":[{"t":"the Eleven","d":"Athenian officials in charge of prisons and executions."},{"t":"kottabos","d":"A drinking game of flinging the last drops of wine, which Theramenes mimics with the hemlock."}]},{"i":310,"j":331,"loc":"2.4.1–2.4.22","title":"Thrasybulus at Phyle and Munichia","summary":"The Thirty expel those off the roll from the city, and Thrasybulus seizes Phyle with seventy men; a snowstorm turns back the attack on it, and his force surprises the guards at dawn. The Thirty seize and condemn the men of Eleusis, making the Three Thousand share the guilt by open vote. Thrasybulus occupies Piraeus and, on the hill of Munichia, exhorts his men that the gods fight for the wronged; the seer charges first and dies as foretold. Critias and Charmides fall, and Cleocritus, herald of the initiated, appeals to the city's men by shared rites and kinship to abandon the Thirty.","ideas":["Phyle as base of the democratic exiles","Eleusinians seized and condemned by public ballot","Victory uphill at Munichia; Critias killed","Cleocritus' plea against civil war"],"terms":[{"t":"Munichia","d":"The hill in Piraeus where the men from Phyle defeated the Thirty."},{"t":"paean","d":"The battle hymn raised before the charge, with an invocation of Enyalius."}]},{"i":332,"j":352,"loc":"2.4.23–2.4.43","title":"Pausanias and the Athenian reconciliation","summary":"The Three Thousand depose the Thirty, who retreat to Eleusis, and choose the Ten, while the Piraeus party grows and promises equal taxation to foreign allies. Lysander secures a loan and an army to blockade Piraeus, but King Pausanias, jealous of him, leads out a Spartan army himself, fights an indecisive battle, and then secretly encourages both Athenian parties to seek peace. Spartan commissioners arrange a reconciliation excluding the Thirty, the Eleven, and the Ten of Piraeus, with Eleusis open to the fearful. Thrasybulus tells the city party to 'know themselves' but keep their oaths; the Eleusis faction is later absorbed, and the amnesty holds to Xenophon's day.","ideas":["Rivalry of Pausanias and Lysander decides Athens' fate","Battle near Piraeus and secret peace overtures","Terms of reconciliation and Eleusis as refuge","The amnesty: not remembering past grievances"],"terms":[{"t":"the Ten","d":"The board chosen to replace the Thirty in the city."},{"t":"amnesty","d":"The sworn pledge not to recall past wrongs, which Xenophon says the people still keep."}]},{"i":353,"j":381,"loc":"3.1.1–3.1.28","title":"Thibron, Dercylidas, and the fall of Meidias","summary":"After referring Cyrus' expedition to an account by Themistogenes, Xenophon tells how the Ionian cities, threatened by Tissaphernes, ask Sparta for protection. Thibron campaigns timidly until joined by Cyrus' returned Greeks, fails at Larisa and is exiled for plundering allies; Dercylidas, nicknamed 'Sisyphus', replaces him and turns on Pharnabazus. Mania, widow of Zenis, ruled Aeolis ably for Pharnabazus until her son-in-law Meidias murdered her. Dercylidas wins the three coastal cities in a day, takes Cebren after four days of unfavourable sacrifices, frees Scepsis, outmanoeuvres Meidias at Gergis, and seizes Mania's treasure as pay for his army.","ideas":["Sparta takes up the cause of the Asian Greeks","Thibron's failure and Dercylidas' resourcefulness","Mania's rule and murder","Dercylidas claims Mania's wealth as Pharnabazus' property"],"terms":[{"t":"satrap","d":"A Persian provincial governor; Mania holds Aeolis as satrap under Pharnabazus."},{"t":"Cyreans","d":"The Greek mercenaries who had marched with Cyrus and then joined the Spartan army."}]},{"i":382,"j":412,"loc":"3.2.1–3.2.31","title":"Dercylidas' truces and Sparta's war on Elis","summary":"Dercylidas agrees a truce with Pharnabazus and winters in Bithynia, where a guard of two hundred Greeks is massacred by Bithynian light troops. He walls the Chersonese isthmus against the Thracians, takes Atarneus, and when ordered into Caria meets the combined army of Tissaphernes and Pharnabazus; Tissaphernes, wary of Greeks like Cyrus' men, seeks a conference and a truce pending terms. Meanwhile Sparta, angry at Elis for excluding it from the Olympic games and scourging Lichas, invades twice; the first invasion halts at an earthquake, the second ravages Elis. Elis gives up its outlying towns but keeps the presidency of Olympia.","ideas":["Massacre of the hoplite guard in Bithynia","Chersonese wall built in one season","Standoff in the Maeander plain and truce","Elean war ends with Elis stripped of its dependent towns"],"terms":[{"t":"the ban (phroura)","d":"The Spartan call-up of the army for a campaign, ordered by the ephors."}]},{"i":413,"j":423,"loc":"3.3.1–3.3.11","title":"Agesilaus made king; Cinadon's conspiracy","summary":"After Agis dies, his supposed son Leotychides and his brother Agesilaus dispute the throne; Diopeithes cites an oracle against a 'lame kingship' aimed at the lame Agesilaus, but Lysander reads it as warning against a king not of Heracles' line, and Agesilaus is chosen. Soon ominous sacrifices are followed by the report of a plot led by Cinadon, who is not one of the peers. The informer says Cinadon counted the few Spartiates in the market as enemies and the many others as allies who would gladly eat them raw. The ephors send Cinadon to Aulon on a pretext, arrest him there, learn his confederates, and punish them; he says he wished to be inferior to no one.","ideas":["Disputed succession and the 'lame kingship' oracle","Few Spartiates amid many resentful subjects","Tools of labour as weapons","Covert arrest of Cinadon"],"terms":[{"t":"peers (homoioi)","d":"Full Spartan citizens, a small privileged body."},{"t":"Perioeci and Helots","d":"Free non-citizen dwellers of Laconia and the state serfs, counted by Cinadon among the discontented."}]},{"i":424,"j":452,"loc":"3.4.1–3.4.29","title":"Agesilaus' campaign in Asia","summary":"News of a Persian fleet prompts Lysander to persuade Agesilaus to lead an army to Asia, partly hoping to restore his own decarchies; at Aulis the Boeotarchs break up Agesilaus' sacrifice. At Ephesus Tissaphernes swears a truce and breaks it, while Agesilaus humiliates Lysander, who has drawn the petitioners' attention, and sends him to the Hellespont, where he wins over Spithridates. Agesilaus feints toward Caria, raids Phrygia, raises cavalry, trains his army at Ephesus, and then marches on Sardis, defeating the Persian horse on the Pactolus. Tissaphernes is beheaded on the King's orders; Tithraustes pays Agesilaus to move into Pharnabazus' province, and Agesilaus is given command of the fleet.","ideas":["Agesilaus' aim: autonomy of the Asian cities","Rivalry with Lysander resolved in Agesilaus' favour","Ephesus as a workshop of war; captives shown naked","Victory near Sardis and execution of Tissaphernes"],"terms":[{"t":"decarchies","d":"Ten-man oligarchic boards Lysander set up in the cities, overthrown by the ephors."},{"t":"Boeotarchs","d":"The chief magistrates of the Boeotian confederacy."}]},{"i":453,"j":477,"loc":"3.5.1–3.5.25","title":"Persian gold and the battle of Haliartus","summary":"Tithraustes sends Timocrates of Rhodes with gold to stir Thebes, Corinth and Argos against Sparta, and Theban leaders provoke a Locrian-Phocian clash that brings Sparta into Boeotia. Theban envoys at Athens argue that Spartan rule is resented by all and easier to overthrow than Athens' empire was, and the Athenians vote unanimously to help. Lysander reaches Haliartus before Pausanias, attacks the wall, and is killed, though his men inflict losses on the pursuing Thebans. Pausanias, facing the Athenians and Thebans, recovers the dead under truce and withdraws; condemned at home, he flees to Tegea.","ideas":["Persian bribes behind the anti-Spartan coalition","Theban speech: Sparta's allies now its enemies","Death of Lysander at Haliartus","Pausanias' truce and condemnation"],"terms":[{"t":"Haliartus","d":"Boeotian town where Lysander fell attacking the wall."}]},{"i":478,"j":519,"loc":"4.1.1–4.1.41","title":"Agesilaus in Phrygia and his meeting with Pharnabazus","summary":"Agesilaus ravages Pharnabazus' province of Phrygia, wins the Paphlagonian king Otys as an ally through Spithridates, and arranges a marriage between Otys and Spithridates' daughter. Wintering at Dascyleium, his foragers are routed by Pharnabazus' scythed chariots, but Herippidas then captures the satrap's camp; his seizure of the booty drives Spithridates and the Paphlagonians to desert. Apollophanes of Cyzicus brings Pharnabazus to a parley on the grass, where the satrap reproaches the Spartans for ruining a loyal friend and Agesilaus invites him to freedom as their ally. Pharnabazus answers that he will fight for the King if given the command; Agesilaus promises to leave his land, exchanges gifts with his young son, and prepares a march inland in spring.","ideas":["Agesilaus aims to detach peoples from the Persian King","Herippidas' greed over booty costs Sparta its Asian allies","Pharnabazus' complaint of ingratitude and Agesilaus' offer of freedom over servitude","ambition (philotimia) keeps Pharnabazus loyal to the King"],"terms":[{"t":"Spartiatae","d":"Full Spartan citizens; thirty of them accompany Agesilaus as his council."},{"t":"xenia (guest-friendship)","d":"A bond of hospitality between individuals of different states, which Agesilaus says yields to war between the states."}]},{"i":520,"j":542,"loc":"4.2.1–4.2.23","title":"Agesilaus recalled; the battle of Nemea","summary":"Alarmed that Persian money has united the largest Greek states against them, the Lacedaemonians recall Agesilaus, who leaves Euxenus in Asia and offers prizes to recruit the best troops for the march home along Xerxes' route. At home Aristodemus leads the army for the boy-king Agesipolis. The allied council hears Timolaus of Corinth urge them to strike Sparta near its source, like a river or a wasps' nest, but they lose time disputing command and depth of ranks. At the river near Nemea the Boeotians deepen their phalanx and veer right; the Spartans outflank the Athenians, roll up the returning Argives, Corinthians and Thebans on their unshielded sides, and set up a trophy.","ideas":["Persian gold behind the anti-Spartan coalition","Timolaus' images of the river and the wasps' nest","both phalanxes drift rightward; the Spartan wing wins while their allies are beaten","the Spartans strike the enemy's unprotected flanks as they return from pursuit"],"terms":[{"t":"ephors","d":"Sparta's annual board of magistrates, who call out the levy and direct the kings."},{"t":"phalanx","d":"The close-packed battle line of hoplites, whose depth and drift to the right decide the battle."}]},{"i":543,"j":565,"loc":"4.3.1–4.3.23","title":"Thessalian cavalry, Cnidus, and the battle of Coronea","summary":"On the march home Agesilaus learns of the Nemea victory and sends Dercylidas to announce it in Asia. In Thessaly he routs the famed Thessalian horse with his own new cavalry and sets up a trophy. At the edge of Boeotia, during an eclipse, he hears that Peisander has been killed and the fleet destroyed by Conon and Pharnabazus off Cnidos; he tells his troops instead that the Spartans won. At Coronea, which Xenophon calls unlike any battle of his time, Agesilaus' wing routs the Argives, then he meets the Thebans head-on rather than letting them pass, and wins though badly wounded; he spares suppliants in Athena's temple and dedicates a tithe at Delphi, while Gylis is killed in a raid on Locris.","ideas":["Agesilaus' cavalry defeats the proudest horsemen of Greece","the naval defeat at Cnidos concealed from the troops","at Coronea Agesilaus chooses the frontal clash over the safer course","piety toward suppliants in Athena's temple"],"terms":[{"t":"polemarch","d":"A Spartan commander of a regiment (mora), such as Gylis."},{"t":"trophy (tropaion)","d":"A monument of captured arms set up on the field by the side holding it after a battle."}]},{"i":566,"j":584,"loc":"4.4.1–4.4.19","title":"Massacre at Corinth and the fight within the Long Walls","summary":"With the war based on Corinth, Corinthians who want peace are massacred during the festival of the Euclea by the war party and its allies, in what Xenophon calls utter sacrilege; Corinth is then merged with Argos. Survivors led by Pasimelus and Alcimenes admit the Spartan Praxitas inside the walls running to Lechaeum. The Argives break the Sicyonians, but the Spartans catch them in the confined space, where they are slaughtered in heaps, which Xenophon calls a gift from heaven. Praxitas breaches the walls; thereafter mercenaries fight the war, Iphicrates' peltasts terrorize Phlius and Arcadia, the Athenians rebuild the walls, and Agesilaus and Teleutias retake them.","ideas":["the impious massacre at a religious festival","Corinth absorbed into Argos, its boundary stones removed","Pasimachus' Sigma shields ruse and death","the rise of mercenary peltasts under Iphicrates"],"terms":[{"t":"peltasts","d":"Light-armed javelin troops with small shields, effective against hoplites in Iphicrates' hands."},{"t":"Long Walls","d":"The walls joining Corinth to its port Lechaeum, the scene of fighting here."}]},{"i":585,"j":603,"loc":"4.5.1–4.5.19","title":"Piraeum taken and the regiment lost at Lechaeum","summary":"Agesilaus disrupts the Argives' Isthmian games, so they are held twice, then seizes Piraeum, where his men on the cold heights are revived by fire he sends up. While he sits watching the prisoners and snubbing Boeotian envoys, news comes that the Spartan regiment at Lechaeum has been destroyed. Xenophon explains how: after escorting the Amyclaeans home for the Hyacinthia, about six hundred hoplites were picked off by Iphicrates' peltasts, whom their sallies could never catch, until about two hundred and fifty fell. Agesilaus withdraws, avoiding Mantinea at night, and Iphicrates goes on to retake the Spartan outposts.","ideas":["the Isthmian games celebrated twice in one year","Agesilaus' exultation cut short by bad news","hoplites helpless against peltasts who retreat and return","kinsmen of the fallen bear themselves like victors"],"terms":[{"t":"mora (regiment)","d":"A Spartan army division of several hundred hoplites; the one at Lechaeum is destroyed."},{"t":"year-classes","d":"Age groups of soldiers counted from military age; the younger ones are sent out to pursue."}]},{"i":604,"j":624,"loc":"4.6.1–4.7.7","title":"Agesilaus in Acarnania; Agesipolis invades Argos","summary":"Threatened with Achaean defection, Sparta sends Agesilaus against the Acarnanians; he advances slowly, then makes a sudden long march to seize their herds, and fights his way out of a mountain pass. He refuses the Achaeans' wish to stop the sowing, reasoning that people with crops to lose will want peace, and the Acarnanians do make peace the next spring. Agesipolis, after Zeus at Olympia and Apollo at Delphi approve ignoring the Argives' unjust plea of sacred months, invades Argos. He treats an earthquake as encouragement and tries to outdo Agesilaus' raid, but withdraws after a thunderbolt and a lobeless liver.","ideas":["slow ravaging lulls the enemy before a swift strike","letting the enemy sow makes them desire peace","the Argive plea of a holy truce rejected with oracular backing","omens read by timing: an earthquake after invasion urges on"],"terms":[{"t":"sacred months (holy truce)","d":"Festival periods when war was forbidden; the Argives plead them whenever invaded."},{"t":"ban (levy)","d":"The calling out of Sparta's full citizen army with its allies."}]},{"i":625,"j":643,"loc":"4.8.1–4.8.19","title":"Conon rebuilds Athens' walls; Antalcidas' first peace attempt","summary":"After Cnidus, Pharnabazus and Conon expel Spartan governors and promise the coastal cities independence, but Dercylidas holds Abydus and Sestus by appeals to loyalty in misfortune. In spring Pharnabazus raids Laconia, garrisons Cythera, and funds the allies at Corinth; Conon then rebuilds the Long Walls of Athens with Persian money. Sparta sends Antalcidas to Tiribazus offering the Asian cities to the King and independence for all others; the Athenians, Thebans and Argives refuse, but Tiribazus secretly funds Sparta and imprisons Conon. Struthas succeeds Tiribazus and favours Athens, and Thibron is killed in a careless raid while throwing the discus.","ideas":["Dercylidas: loyalty shown in adversity is remembered","Persian money rebuilds the walls Sparta had torn down","each rival fears what independence of cities would cost it","Thibron's disorder brings his death"],"terms":[{"t":"harmost (governor)","d":"A Spartan governor placed in an allied or subject city."},{"t":"autonomy (independence)","d":"The right of each city to govern itself, the core term of Antalcidas' proposal."}]},{"i":644,"j":663,"loc":"4.8.20–4.8.39","title":"Rhodes, Thrasybulus' last voyage, and Anaxibius' death","summary":"Rhodian oligarchic exiles draw Sparta into Rhodes; Diphridas fights Struthas successfully, and Teleutias takes over the fleet and captures Athenian ships bound for Euagoras, an act Xenophon notes ran against both sides' interests. Thrasybulus sails to the Hellespont, reconciles Thracian rulers, levies the Byzantine toll, makes Byzantium democratic, and, after killing Therimachus at Methymna, brings over some Lesbian cities, but is killed at Aspendus after his men plunder. Anaxibius is sent to Abydus and harasses the Athenians; Iphicrates ambushes him on a mountain descent, and Anaxibius chooses to die fighting with about twelve governors.","ideas":["factional struggle at Rhodes tied to Athenian or Spartan power","Athens aids Euagoras against its Persian friend while Sparta strikes Athens","Thrasybulus restores Athenian influence in the Hellespont","Iphicrates' ambush at Cremaste"],"terms":[{"t":"tithe-duty","d":"A ten percent toll on ships leaving the Black Sea, sold at Byzantium by Thrasybulus."}]},{"i":664,"j":688,"loc":"5.1.1–5.1.24","title":"War around Aegina and Teleutias' raid on Piraeus","summary":"From Aegina, raiders licensed by Eteonicus harass Attica; Athens builds a fort there, and Teleutias drives off its fleet and leaves amid his men's affection, which Xenophon thinks worth more notice than costly exploits. Gorgopas ambushes Eunomus by moonlight off Cape Zoster, but is himself killed in Chabrias' ambush on Aegina. When unpaid sailors refuse to row, Teleutias returns and promises to share every hardship with them and to live off the enemy. With twelve triremes he raids Piraeus at dawn, reasoning that crews at home are scattered, seizes merchant ships and men, and pays his sailors a month in advance.","ideas":["Xenophon praises Teleutias' power to win his men's love","night ambushes on both sides at Aegina","Teleutias' speech: shared toil, pay from the enemy","a small fleet strikes the unready harbour of Athens"],"terms":[{"t":"Deigma","d":"The trading hall at Piraeus from which Teleutias' men seize merchants."}]},{"i":689,"j":710,"loc":"5.1.25–5.2.10","title":"The King's Peace; Mantinea split into villages","summary":"Antalcidas returns with the King's alliance, captures Thrasybulus of Collytus' squadron, and with over eighty ships controls the sea, so that war-weary Athens, Sparta and Argos all want peace. Tiribazus reads Artaxerxes' rescript: Asia and Cyprus to the King, Lemnos, Imbros and Scyros to Athens, all other cities independent. Agesilaus forces Thebes to free the Boeotian cities and the Argives to leave Corinth; Xenophon says the Peace of Antalcidas raised Sparta's standing. Sparta then punishes disloyal allies, flooding Mantinea's wall with the dammed river and dispersing the city into four villages, and makes Phlius restore its pro-Spartan exiles.","ideas":["the King dictates the peace terms to the Greeks","independence of cities used by Sparta to break up Boeotia and Argive Corinth","Mantinea's wall undermined by its own river","discipline shown in sparing the Mantinean democrats"],"terms":[{"t":"Peace of Antalcidas (King's Peace)","d":"The settlement sent down by Artaxerxes, with Sparta as its enforcer."},{"t":"dioikismos","d":"Dispersal of a city into its constituent villages, imposed on Mantinea."}]},{"i":711,"j":743,"loc":"5.2.11–5.2.43","title":"Olynthus feared; Phoebidas seizes the Cadmea","summary":"Cleigenes of Acanthus warns Sparta and its allies that Olynthus is building a powerful league in the north and must be checked before its bonds harden; an army of ten thousand is voted, with money allowed in place of men. Eudamidas goes ahead, and his brother Phoebidas, passing Thebes, is persuaded by the polemarch Leontiades to seize the Cadmea during the Thesmophoria; Leontiades arrests his rival Ismenias, and some three hundred opponents flee to Athens. Agesilaus argues the act should be judged by its benefit, Sparta keeps the citadel, and Ismenias is tried and executed. Teleutias then leads the joint army against Olynthus and wins a cavalry fight with Derdas' help.","ideas":["the Olynthian league as a growing danger to Spartan order","a seizure in peacetime of the Theban acropolis","Agesilaus judges the deed by advantage, not authorization","Ismenias condemned as a partisan of Persia"],"terms":[{"t":"Cadmea","d":"The acropolis of Thebes, seized and garrisoned by Phoebidas."},{"t":"sympoliteia","d":"Shared citizenship among cities, the basis of the Olynthian league."}]},{"i":744,"j":770,"loc":"5.3.1–5.3.27","title":"Teleutias' death, Phlius besieged, Olynthus submits","summary":"Derdas routs Olynthian raiders, but Teleutias, angered by enemy cavalry, attacks rashly near the walls and is killed with much of his army; Xenophon draws the lesson that anger, unlike judgment, does not look ahead. Agesipolis is sent north, ravages Olynthian land and takes Torone, but dies of fever at Aphytis, and Agesilaus mourns him. Agesilaus besieges Phlius on behalf of its restored exiles, training a force of their sympathizers; the Phliasians, rationing food and led by Delphion, hold out twice as long as expected before surrendering after a year and eight months. Olynthus, starved, becomes Sparta's ally, and Spartan power seems firmly established.","ideas":["anger versus judgment in war","Agesilaus' grief for his fellow king","self-restraint lets Phlius endure a long siege","the high point of Spartan hegemony"],"terms":[{"t":"Perioeci","d":"Free non-citizen inhabitants of Laconia who served in Spartan armies."}]},{"i":771,"j":803,"loc":"5.4.1–5.4.33","title":"Thebes liberated; Sphodrias' raid and acquittal","summary":"Xenophon presents the liberation of Thebes as divine punishment of Sparta for seizing the Cadmea despite its oath. Phillidas and Melon with six exiles, disguised as women, kill the polemarchs at their feast, then Leontiades, free the prisoners and rouse the city; with Athenian help the Spartan garrison surrenders, and its commander is later executed. Cleombrotus' winter campaign achieves little. The Thebans, it was suspected, bribe Sphodrias to attempt a night attack on Piraeus, which fails at dawn; through Archidamus' love for Sphodrias' son Cleonymus, Agesilaus relents, saying Sparta needs such soldiers, and Sphodrias is acquitted, a verdict Xenophon reports many thought most unjust.","ideas":["the gods punish broken oaths","the banquet assassination of the Theban polemarchs","Sphodrias' botched attack on Piraeus","Cleonymus later keeps his promise by dying at Leuctra"],"terms":[{"t":"polemarchs (Theban)","d":"Chief magistrates of Thebes, here the pro-Spartan rulers killed by the exiles."},{"t":"proxenos","d":"A citizen who represents another state's interests, like Callias for Sparta at Athens."}]},{"i":804,"j":836,"loc":"5.4.34–5.4.66","title":"Agesilaus' Boeotian campaigns and the war at sea","summary":"Angered by Sphodrias' acquittal, Athens gates Piraeus, builds ships and backs Thebes. Agesilaus twice invades Boeotia, seizing Cithaeron in advance and slipping past the Theban stockade by surprise, but achieves no decisive result; Phoebidas is killed pursuing Thebans near Thespiae, and the Thebans recover their spirit and the nearby cities. Alcetas loses Oreus through carelessness, and Agesilaus falls ill after a ruptured vein. Cleombrotus is turned back at Cithaeron; Sparta's fleet under Pollis is beaten by Chabrias, and Timotheus sails round Peloponnesus, wins Corcyra without enslaving or exiling anyone, defeats Nicolochus at Alyzeia, and keeps sending to Athens for money.","ideas":["the Theban stockade and Agesilaus' feints","Phoebidas' death revives Theban confidence","Athens regains the sea under Chabrias and Timotheus","Timotheus' mild treatment of Corcyra wins goodwill"],"terms":[{"t":"Sciritans","d":"Light troops from the Sciritis district on Laconia's border, serving in the Spartan army."}]},{"i":837,"j":856,"loc":"6.1.1–6.1.19","title":"Polydamas warns Sparta about Jason of Pherae","summary":"As Cleombrotus is sent to Phocis against the Thebans, Polydamas of Pharsalus comes to the Lacedaemonian assembly. He reports Jason's offer: with his trained mercenaries and most Thessalian cities on his side, Jason means to become Tagus of Thessaly, then win sea power and even conquer the Persian King, and would rather win Pharsalus by persuasion than force. Polydamas describes Jason's endurance, speed and self-control and asks for an adequate force. The Lacedaemonians, counting their regiments, say they cannot send one; Polydamas gives Jason his children as hostages and Jason is made Tagus, assessing large levies of cavalry, hoplites and peltasts.","ideas":["Jason's mercenary army rewarded by merit","Jason's plan: Thessaly, sea power, then Persia","Sparta admits it cannot spare an adequate force","Jason becomes Tagus by common consent"],"terms":[{"t":"Tagus","d":"The office of supreme military leader of united Thessaly."},{"t":"proxenos (diplomatic agent)","d":"A citizen who represents another state's interests in his own city, as Polydamas does for Sparta."}]},{"i":857,"j":895,"loc":"6.2.1–6.2.39","title":"The siege of Corcyra and Iphicrates' voyage","summary":"Athens makes peace with Sparta, but Timotheus lands Zacynthian exiles on the way home, and Sparta sends Mnasippus with sixty ships against Corcyra. He ravages the island and blockades the starving city, but, withholding pay and striking his captains, leads disaffected mercenaries into a sally in which he is killed; his fleet flees to Leucas. Athens replaces the slow Timotheus with Iphicrates, whose voyage round the Peloponnese Xenophon praises for training his crews in manoeuvres while sailing fast. At Corcyra Iphicrates captures Dionysius's relief squadron, then campaigns in Acarnania and Cephallenia, and asks for Callistratus and Chabrias as colleagues.","ideas":["Mnasippus's mistreatment of mercenaries leads to his defeat and death","Iphicrates combines training and speed on the voyage","Capture of the Syracusan triremes at Corcyra","Xenophon commends Iphicrates' choice of colleagues"],"terms":[{"t":"trireme","d":"The standard Greek warship, rowed by three banks of oarsmen."}]},{"i":896,"j":915,"loc":"6.3.1–6.3.20","title":"The peace congress at Sparta, 371","summary":"Offended by Thebes' destruction of Plataea and Thespiae and its attacks on Phocis, Athens sends envoys to Sparta to make peace. Callias appeals to his family's role as peacemakers and the shared legend of Triptolemus and Demeter's gifts; Autocles bluntly charges Sparta with violating the independence it proclaims, citing its decarchies and the seizure of the Cadmea. Callistratus argues that both sides have erred and learned, that together they would be unassailable, and that one should stop while still strong. Peace is sworn on the terms of withdrawing governors and leaving cities independent; when Thebes demands to sign for 'the Boeotians', Agesilaus refuses, and Thebes is left outside the treaty.","ideas":["Autocles accuses Sparta of hypocrisy about autonomy","Callistratus: make friends while still strong, like a wise athlete or gambler","Thebes excluded over signing for all Boeotia"],"terms":[{"t":"autonomia (independence)","d":"The right of each city to govern itself, the professed basis of the King's Peace."},{"t":"Cadmea","d":"The citadel of Thebes, which Sparta had seized in peacetime."}]},{"i":916,"j":930,"loc":"6.4.1–6.4.15","title":"The battle of Leuctra","summary":"Athens withdraws its garrisons, but the Spartan assembly, rejecting Prothous's advice to disband, orders Cleombrotus to march on Thebes. He enters Boeotia by an unexpected route and camps at Leuctra; friends warn him he will be punished if he does not fight, while the Theban leaders judge it better to die fighting than be exiled again, encouraged by omens. The Theban cavalry routs the poor Spartan horse, and the Theban column fifty shields deep breaks the Spartan right; Cleombrotus is carried off mortally wounded. With some four hundred of seven hundred Spartiatae dead and the allies unwilling, the polemarchs ask for the dead under truce.","ideas":["Sparta refuses to disband, led on by fate","Thebans massed fifty deep against a twelve-deep line","Spartan cavalry weak because of how it was raised","Decisive Spartan defeat and request for a truce"],"terms":[{"t":"polemarch","d":"A senior Spartan officer commanding a regiment (mora)."},{"t":"phalanx","d":"The close-ordered line of hoplites; its depth is decisive at Leuctra."}]},{"i":931,"j":952,"loc":"6.4.16–6.4.37","title":"After Leuctra: Sparta's grief, Jason's rise and fall","summary":"News of Leuctra reaches Sparta during the Gymnopaediae; the ephors let the chorus finish, and kinsmen of the dead go about cheerfully. Archidamus leads a relief army while Athens coldly receives the Theban herald. Jason hurries to Boeotia, dissuades the Thebans from a second battle and arranges a truce, perhaps so both sides would need him; the Spartans withdraw by night. Back in Thessaly, Jason plans to preside over the Pythian festival but is murdered by seven young men, honoured afterwards in Greek cities. His successors Polydorus, Polyphron and the cruel Alexander are each killed in turn, Alexander by his wife's brothers at her instigation.","ideas":["Spartan restraint in public grief","Jason's speed and his self-interested mediation","Jason's murder shows Greek fear of a tyranny","A chain of murders among the rulers of Pherae"],"terms":[{"t":"Gymnopaediae","d":"A Spartan summer festival with choral performances."},{"t":"ephors","d":"The five annual Spartan magistrates who direct state affairs."}]},{"i":953,"j":973,"loc":"6.5.1–6.5.21","title":"Mantinea rebuilt; Agesilaus invades Arcadia","summary":"Athens convenes the cities to swear to the King's Peace and to aid one another; only Elis refuses. The Mantineans reunite as one walled city despite Agesilaus's embassy. At Tegea the party of Callibius and Proxenus, seeking Arcadian union, defeats Stasippus's party with Mantinean help and executes those who took refuge in the temple of Artemis. Sparta sends Agesilaus against Mantinea; he treats Eutaea with restraint, withdraws his army skilfully from a valley, and waits three days in midwinter while the Arcadians, awaiting the Thebans, refuse battle, then returns home.","ideas":["Oath at Athens on the King's Peace","Mantinea's synoecism and walls","Civil strife at Tegea over Arcadian union","Agesilaus ravages Mantinean land unopposed"],"terms":[{"t":"Arcadian League","d":"The new federal union of Arcadian cities, with a common assembly."}]},{"i":974,"j":984,"loc":"6.5.22–6.5.32","title":"The Theban invasion of Laconia","summary":"The Thebans arrive and, urged by Arcadians, Argives and Eleans and by reports of Sparta's lack of men, decide to invade Laconia. The Arcadians overwhelm Ischolaus's garrison at Oeum, and the allies descend, burning Sellasia and plundering along the Eurotas. Sparta, unwalled and thinly manned, frees Helots who will serve, then fears their numbers until allied help arrives. An ambush of young hoplites and a cavalry charge near the sanctuary of Poseidon turn back the enemy horse, and the invaders move south to burn unwalled towns and attack Gytheium.","ideas":["Perioeci offer to revolt","Ischolaus falls at Oeum","Spartan women see enemy smoke for the first time","Helots enrolled with a promise of freedom"],"terms":[{"t":"Perioeci","d":"The free but non-citizen inhabitants of the towns of Laconia."},{"t":"Helots","d":"The state-owned serfs of Sparta."}]},{"i":985,"j":1004,"loc":"6.5.33–6.5.52","title":"Athens votes to save Sparta","summary":"Lacedaemonian envoys at Athens recall past cooperation and their refusal to destroy Athens in 404, and invoke the oaths. Cleiteles of Corinth points out that Corinth has wronged no one yet is ravaged, while Procles of Phlius argues that Thebes would turn on Athens next, that one fears those one injures, not those one benefits, and that saving Sparta would be nobler than the ancestral deeds Athens celebrates. Athens votes to march in full force under Iphicrates. The invaders, short of supplies in winter, withdraw, and Xenophon criticizes Iphicrates for leaving the pass by Cenchreae unguarded and sending too many horsemen to scout, so that the Thebans return home unhindered.","ideas":["Procles: aiding Sparta is aiding Athens","Appeal to Thermopylae and Athenian tradition of helping the wronged","Iphicrates' faulty generalship at Oneum"],"terms":[]},{"i":1005,"j":1027,"loc":"Book VII, 7.1.1–7.1.22","title":"Alliance terms; fighting at Oneum and Corinth","summary":"Envoys at Athens settle the Athenian–Spartan alliance. Procles proposes that Athens lead by sea and Sparta by land, as nature and the gods have arranged; Cephisodotus objects that Athenians would then command Helots and mercenaries while Spartans commanded Athenian citizens, and Athens votes for alternating command every five days. The Thebans surprise the Spartan guard at Oneum at dawn and the polemarch makes a truce; they then attack Sicyon, Pellene and Epidaurus, but are repulsed near Corinth. Dionysius's Celtic and Iberian horsemen harass the Theban army with skirmishing tactics before returning to Syracuse.","ideas":["Leadership by sea and by land debated","Command alternated every five days","Dionysius's mounted skirmishers control the Theban army's movements"],"terms":[{"t":"hēgemonia (leadership)","d":"Command of an alliance's forces, here divided or alternated between Athens and Sparta."}]},{"i":1028,"j":1045,"loc":"7.1.23–7.1.40","title":"Arcadian pride, Archidamus's victory, Pelopidas at Susa","summary":"Lycomedes of Mantinea tells the Arcadians they are the native, most numerous and bravest people of the Peloponnese and should not follow Thebes blindly; their successes make them proud, and Thebes and Elis grow unfriendly. A peace conference at Delphi fails over Messene. Archidamus, with Dionysius's second force, routs the Arcadians and Argives without losing a man, and Sparta weeps for joy. Pelopidas then wins the Persian King's favour, obtaining a rescript that Messene be independent and Athens beach its fleet; Timagoras is executed at Athens, Antiochus scorns the King's power, and the cities refuse to swear to the rescript at Thebes.","ideas":["Lycomedes stirs Arcadian independence from Thebes","The battle where no Spartan fell","Pelopidas's embassy to the King","The cities, led by Corinth, refuse the King's terms"],"terms":[{"t":"Ten Thousand","d":"The federal assembly of the Arcadian League."}]},{"i":1046,"j":1074,"loc":"7.1.41–7.2.23","title":"Euphron's tyranny and the loyalty of Phlius","summary":"Epaminondas takes Achaea by arrangement with its aristocrats, but Thebes then installs democracies; the exiles return and join Sparta. At Sicyon Euphron sets up a democracy with Argive and Arcadian backing and makes himself tyrant with mercenaries and seized funds. Xenophon then tells of little Phlius, loyal to Sparta after Leuctra: it reaches Sparta during the invasion, routs the Argive rearguard, repels exiles who seize its acropolis, defeats Theban, Sicyonian and Pellenean forces, and releases a prisoner without ransom. Starving, the Phliasians bring in supplies with Chares and, with him, capture the Sicyonian fort at Thyamia.","ideas":["Euphron rises to tyranny through democracy","A small state's noble deeds deserve record","Recapture of the Phliasian acropolis","Seizure of Thyamia with Chares"],"terms":[{"t":"tyrannos (tyrant)","d":"One who holds sole power by force, as Euphron does at Sicyon."}]},{"i":1075,"j":1086,"loc":"7.3.1–7.3.12","title":"The fall and murder of Euphron","summary":"When Aeneas of Stymphalus restores Sicyonian aristocrats, Euphron hands the port to Sparta, claiming he was always loyal. He later returns with Athenian mercenaries and goes to Thebes with money to buy control of the city, where exiles kill him on the Acropolis during a council session. Arraigned by the Theban officials, one killer defends the deed: Euphron robbed temples, betrayed both sides, made himself tyrant, and came to corrupt Thebes with bribes, and traitors and tyrants have no truce. The Thebans acquit him, but the Sicyonians bury Euphron in their market-place and honour him as founder.","ideas":["Euphron's double desertion","Defence of tyrannicide before the Theban council","People call their benefactors good men"],"terms":[]},{"i":1087,"j":1097,"loc":"7.4.1–7.4.11","title":"Oropus lost; Corinth and Phlius make peace","summary":"Exiles seize Oropus and Athens, unaided by its allies, leaves it to Thebes. Lycomedes negotiates an Arcadian alliance with Athens and is then killed when he lands among Arcadian exiles. When Athens talks of securing Corinth, the Corinthians dismiss the Athenian garrisons and refuse Chares' fleet, raise mercenaries, and ask Thebes for peace. With Sparta's leave, though Sparta itself will fight on rather than give up Messene, Corinth, Phlius and others make peace with Thebes, refusing an alliance as merely an exchange of war. Phlius leaves Thyamia, but Argos keeps Tricaranum.","ideas":["Athens allies with Arcadia","Corinth expels Athenian garrisons","Sparta refuses to give up Messene","Peace on terms of each keeping its own territory"],"terms":[]},{"i":1098,"j":1118,"loc":"7.4.12–7.4.32","title":"War of Elis and Arcadia; battle at Olympia","summary":"Elis seizes Lasion from the Arcadian League, is defeated, and loses Olympia and Margana; strife between democrats and oligarchs in Elis drives exiles to Pylus. Sparta joins Elis, but Archidamus is wounded at Cromnus, a truce follows, and over a hundred Spartiatae and Perioeci are later captured there. Elis recovers Pylus and Margana. At the Olympic festival held by the Arcadians and Pisatans, the Eleans attack in arms during the pentathlon, rout the Arcadians and Argives, and are driven back only by missiles from the temple roofs.","ideas":["Arcadians occupy Olympia","Archidamus wounded at Cromnus","Fighting in the sacred precinct during the games","Elean valour seemingly inspired by a god"],"terms":[{"t":"Epariti","d":"The standing paid troops of the Arcadian League."},{"t":"Altis","d":"The sacred precinct of Zeus at Olympia."}]},{"i":1119,"j":1133,"loc":"7.4.33–7.5.7","title":"Sacred funds split Arcadia; Epaminondas marches","summary":"The Mantineans refuse to pay the Epariti from Olympia's sacred treasures, and the Arcadian assembly votes to stop using them; leaders fearing an audit call on Thebes. After peace with Elis is sworn at Tegea, the Theban governor and those leaders arrest the aristocrats, then release them when Mantinea protests; Epaminondas declares the arrest right and threatens war. Mantinea, Elis and Achaea conclude that Thebes wants a weak Peloponnese and seek help from Athens and Sparta. Epaminondas marches with Boeotians, Euboeans and Thessalians, waits at Nemea for the Athenians, then moves to Tegea.","ideas":["Use of sacred treasures as a political crisis","The arrests at Tegea","Peloponnesians see Theban designs on them","Phocians refuse to join an offensive war"],"terms":[]},{"i":1134,"j":1153,"loc":"7.5.8–7.5.27","title":"Epaminondas's last campaign and Mantinea","summary":"Xenophon praises Epaminondas's forethought while judging the campaign unfortunate. He marches on nearly empty Sparta, but a Cretan warns Agesilaus, and Archidamus with a hundred men drives back his troops; a cavalry raid on Mantinea is checked by Athenian horsemen. Facing disgrace, Epaminondas seeks battle: his soldiers prepare eagerly, he feigns encamping, then strikes in a massed column like a trireme's prow and routs the enemy. He falls, and his men fail to exploit the victory. Both sides set up trophies, Greece is left in greater confusion than before, and Xenophon ends his history.","ideas":["Archidamus saves Sparta against the odds","Athenian cavalry saves Mantinea's people and herds","The column formation and feigned halt","An indecisive outcome against all expectation"],"terms":[{"t":"tropaion (trophy)","d":"A victory monument on the battlefield; both sides set one up after Mantinea."}]}]});